WEBVTT

longitude-caption.1.1
00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:01.633
How can a clock tell

longitude-caption.1.2
00:00:01.633 --> 00:00:03.133
a ship where it is?

longitude-caption.2.1
00:00:03.433 --> 00:00:04.800
By keeping the time

longitude-caption.2.2
00:00:04.800 --> 00:00:06.867
at a known reference point.

longitude-caption.3.1
00:00:07.100 --> 00:00:08.633
Sailors use the Sun

longitude-caption.3.2
00:00:08.633 --> 00:00:10.400
to find local time,

longitude-caption.3.3
00:00:10.400 --> 00:00:11.867
corrected to match

longitude-caption.3.4
00:00:11.867 --> 00:00:12.967
the clock’s timekeeping.

longitude-caption.4.1
00:00:13.367 --> 00:00:14.433
Compare them

longitude-caption.4.2
00:00:14.433 --> 00:00:16.133
at the same moment.

longitude-caption.5.1
00:00:16.667 --> 00:00:18.133
Earth’s turning links

longitude-caption.5.2
00:00:18.133 --> 00:00:20.333
that time difference
to longitude:

longitude-caption.5.3
00:00:20.333 --> 00:00:22.300
fifteen degrees per hour.

longitude-caption.6.1
00:00:23.000 --> 00:00:24.733
Twelve at the reference.

longitude-caption.6.2
00:00:24.733 --> 00:00:27.100
Two in the afternoon locally.

longitude-caption.7.1
00:00:28.000 --> 00:00:29.833
Two hours ahead.

longitude-caption.7.2
00:00:29.833 --> 00:00:31.367
Thirty degrees east.

longitude-caption.8.1
00:00:32.500 --> 00:00:34.867
Harrison’s H4
helped demonstrate

longitude-caption.8.2
00:00:34.867 --> 00:00:37.300
accurate timekeeping at sea.

longitude-caption.9.1
00:00:37.567 --> 00:00:39.300
Other astronomical methods

longitude-caption.9.2
00:00:39.300 --> 00:00:41.300
remained useful too.

longitude-caption.10.1
00:00:41.600 --> 00:00:43.900
The clock carries time.

longitude-caption.10.2
00:00:43.900 --> 00:00:45.967
Observation and calculation

longitude-caption.10.3
00:00:45.967 --> 00:00:47.167
reveal longitude.
